1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 Which AI runtime actually fits Articulate? That's the question I've been trying to answer for the last few episodes. Hello, I'm Russell and welcome to episode 13 of the Articulate Journal where I'm exploring what it takes to build an AI native architecture from first principles.

2
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:36,000
 Back in episode 10, I opened ADR1 to select an agent runtime. At that point, I had already had a shortlist, Dapper Agents, Templural, Landgraph and Microsoft Agent Framework.

3
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
 It would have been very easy to put those into a comparison table, count the features and pick the winner and move on.

4
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:50,000
 But I didn't actually understand the problem well enough to make that decision, so instead, I left the ADR open.

5
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 The last two episodes have been about figuring out what the runtime actually needs to do.

6
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
 Now I think I have enough information to make that decision.

7
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:12,000
 The interesting thing is that I no longer answering quite the same question I started with. Initially, I was asking which agent runtime should Articulate use.

8
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 But exploring memory changed that. Most of what gets described as agent memory doesn't actually belong to the runtime.

9
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 Architectural knowledge belongs in the knowledge model.

10
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 Conversation history belongs in a conversation capability, embeddings, indexes and caching supports retrieval.

11
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 External systems remain responsible for their own data.

12
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
 The runtime doesn't need all of that, but durable execution changed the question again.

13
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:49,000
 Because there is one thing the runtime really does need to own, the continuity of execution.

14
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:55,000
 If work had already happened, if a tool had already been executed, if an agent is waiting for approval,

15
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 or if a process disappears halfway through, some long-running reasoning, the platform needs to know where that work had reached.

16
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
 So the question became much narrower, but at the same time, much clearer.

17
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:20,000
 I'm not looking for somewhere to store agent memory. I'm looking for a runtime that can reliably coordinate AI native work across time.

18
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:30,000
 That gives me a much better set of criteria. Articulate needs durable execution, long-running workflows, pause and resume,

19
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,000
 human interaction, failure recovery, distributed execution, tool orchestration and observability.

20
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
 And importantly, I don't want this decision to force decisions that I haven't already made yet.

21
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 I haven't decided a cloud provider. I haven't decided the eventual production hosting model.

22
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:56,000
 I don't even know whether the architecture I have today will still be the architecture I need in several episodes from now.

23
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:06,000
 So, I want a runtime that gives Articulate some useful capabilities now without unnecessarily constraining where the platform can evolve.

24
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
 And with that criteria established, I can finally look at the candidates.

25
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 This is where I expected decision to become easier. It didn't.

26
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
 Because once I started evaluating the candidates against the requirements from the previous episodes,

27
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
 several of them could have satisfied what Articulate needs.

28
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
 And some of the strongest options aren't actually single technologies.

29
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 For example, Microsoft agent framework combined with Temporal is extremely compelling.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
 Microsoft agent framework gives me the agent programming model.

31
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 Temporal gives me mature durable execution. Together, they tick every box I've identified.

32
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:57,000
 And personally, there's another attraction. Microsoft agent framework supports C#.

33
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 I've spent a large part of my career working with C# and .NET.

34
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 So this would put Articulate in a technology ecosystem. I know extremely well.

35
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 That's not something I should pretend doesn't influence the decision.

36
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:19,000
 I understand the framework that has value. It reduces implementation risk.

37
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
 It makes it easier for me to understand what's happening beneath the abstractions.

38
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
 And how it would allow me to concentrate more of my effort on the architectural experiments

39
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
 rather than learning the depths of another programming language in the ecosystem at the same time.

40
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:42,000
 So Microsoft agent framework and Temporal is a very credible option.

41
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:52,000
 There's also another Microsoft orientated option. Microsoft agent framework can use durable task for durable execution.

42
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:04,000
 That potentially gives me a more of an integrated stack, an agent programming model, durable execution, C# and a development environment I know well.

43
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 And fewer independent runtime technologies to assemble. That's attractive, but it also raises an architectural question.

44
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
 How much of that architecture do I want the agent framework itself to own?

45
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:26,000
 The investigation into memory and durable execution has pushed me towards separating responsibilities.

46
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
 Agents perform reasoning. Durable execution preserves the continuity of work.

47
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:39,000
 The surrounding platform owns knowledge, messaging, state and infrastructure concerns.

48
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:45,000
 I don't necessarily want all those things collapsing into a single AI framework simply because it's convenient.

49
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
 Integration has value, but separation of responsibilities has value too.

50
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
 And that's a trade-off rather than an obvious answer.

51
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:02,000
 Landgraph creates another interesting combination. I like its model for representing reasoning.

52
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 And if I combine Landgraph with Temporal, I can separate reasoning orchestration from durable execution.

53
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 Landgraph can concentrate on how the intelligent process reasons.

54
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,000
 Temporal can concentrate on making sure that process survives failure and time.

55
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:28,000
 That's architecturally quite clean. In some ways, it's one of the strongest combinations on the shortlist.

56
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:33,000
 But now, Articulate has two significant runtime abstractions.

57
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:38,000
 I need to understand where Landgraph ends and Temporal begins.

58
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:45,000
 Which system owns orchestration? Which one owns retries? Which one owns execution state?

59
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,000
 And which one determines the life cycle of an agent?

60
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
 There are good answers to those questions.

61
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:58,000
 But every answer introduces another architecture boundary that I have to own.

62
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
 And that leads to another consideration.

63
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:11,000
 At this point, the comparison isn't really dapper versus Temporal versus Landgraph versus Microsoft Agent Framework.

64
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:24,000
 It's closer to Microsoft Agent Framework plus durable task or Microsoft Agent Framework's Temporal or Landgraph plus Temporal or dapper agents.

65
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:29,000
 And potentially, other combinations, all of them can be made to work, and that's important.

66
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:34,000
 I'm not choosing dapper because the alternatives fail the requirements.

67
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:38,000
 I'm choosing between several architectures that satisfy them.

68
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
 So the question becomes one of architectural composition.

69
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:48,000
 How many runtime concepts do I want Articulate to depend upon?

70
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:56,000
 Where do I want the boundaries between agents, workflows and distributed application infrastructure?

71
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:04,000
 And which combination gives me enough capability without assembling more platform than I currently needed?

72
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
 There's another technology decision sitting underneath all of that that I want to make explicit.

73
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
 Dapper agents is currently Python based.

74
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:19,000
 Coming from a C-sharp background that initially makes Microsoft Agent Framework particularly attractive.

75
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:26,000
 But I don't think choosing Python for the AI and reasoning parts of Articulate is actually a compromise.

76
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:33,000
 Python has become a dominant language across machine learning and much of the wider AI ecosystem.

77
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:43,000
 Models, evaluation libraries, data science, tooling, agent frameworks and research implementations frequently appear in Python first.

78
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:48,000
 And Articulate is deliberately an AI native architecture experiment.

79
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:57,000
 There's value in working closer to that ecosystem rather than isolating myself from it simply because C-sharp is more familiar.

80
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:02,000
 That doesn't mean that Articulate suddenly becomes a Python platform.

81
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:07,000
 The architecture shouldn't require every capability to be written in the same language.

82
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:17,000
 If another capability is better implemented using C-sharp, Go or something else in the future, the architecture should allow for that.

83
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:25,000
 But for the parts of Articulate closest to AI, machine learning and reasoning, I think Python is the strongest strategic fit.

84
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:31,000
 So Dapper agents being Python isn't the reason for choosing Python.

85
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,000
 The two decisions simply reinforce each other.

86
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,000
 And after all of that, I still come back to Dapper.

87
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,000
 Not because Dapper agents is obviously better than Microsoft Agent Framework.

88
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:49,000
 Not because Dapper workflows is more capable than temporal.

89
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:53,000
 And not because Dapper has the strongest reasoning abstraction.

90
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
 I don't think I could defend any of those claims.

91
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:00,000
 What attracts me to Dapper is the shape of the platform around the agents.

92
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
 Articulate needs agents and durable workflows.

93
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:13,000
 But it's also going to need messaging, state, service invocation, secrets, observability and ordinary application services.

94
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:19,000
 And integration between all those things, those aren't hypothetical requirements anymore.

95
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
 They're already appearing in the architecture.

96
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:27,000
 Dapper gives me a common distributed application runtime across these concerns.

97
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:35,000
 And its sidecar model gives the application a degree of independence from the infrastructure providing them.

98
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:49,000
 That's particularly valuable because I'm deliberately not deciding yet whether Articulate ultimately runs on Kubernetes, ECS, Azure, AWS, GCP or whatever else.

99
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:59,000
 I'm choosing an execution architecture without unnecessarily choosing the eventual infrastructure architecture at the same time.

100
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:05,000
 And that gives me something I value quite highly at this stage of Articulate options.

101
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
 But that flexibility isn't free.

102
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
 Choosing Dapper means accepting a broader runtime.

103
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
 There's another process running alongside the application.

104
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:22,000
 There are additional operational concepts. I need to understand Dapper itself rather than only understanding the agent framework.

105
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
 And Dapper agents is still relatively young.

106
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
 It's a genuine risk.

107
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:34,000
 There's also a danger of adopting a platform because it provides lots of capabilities that I might need.

108
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:39,000
 That's exactly the kind of reasoning I've tried to avoid throughout this journal.

109
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:45,000
 So the decision can't be, Dapper has more building blocks, therefore Dapper wins.

110
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:54,000
 The question has to remain, do those building blocks correspond to responsibilities actually emerging in the architecture?

111
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
 And for Articulate, I think they do.

112
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:05,000
 Pub/Sub, state, service invocation, observability, durable workflows, distributed execution.

113
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
 These aren't speculative requirements anymore.

114
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
 They are appearing in the architecture.

115
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,000
 So, I'm accepting ADR1.

116
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:25,000
 Articulate will use Dapper agents as its initial agent runtime with Dapper workflows providing durable execution and orchestration.

117
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,000
 I think the word initial matters.

118
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,000
 I'm not saying Dapper is the best agent for runtime.

119
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
 I don't think I've demonstrated that.

120
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:39,000
 What I'm saying is Dapper is currently the best architectural fit for Articulate.

121
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
 That's a much narrower claim.

122
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:45,000
 Microsoft agent framework and temporal remains compelling.

123
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:51,000
 Microsoft agent framework with durable task deserves further experimentation.

124
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:59,000
 And land graph with temporal is particularly interesting if reasoning orchestration becomes significantly more sophisticated.

125
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:10,000
 And temporal remains the benchmark I would come back to if durable execution becomes more demanding than Dapper workflows can comfortably support.

126
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:14,000
 ADR1 isn't declaring those alternatives are wrong.

127
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:18,000
 It's recording why I'm not choosing them now.

128
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:23,000
 And that's what I think an ADR should do.

129
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:29,000
 The most useful part of this investigation isn't actually selecting Dapper.

130
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:33,000
 It was discovering what I was selecting at four.

131
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,000
 I started ADR1 looking for an agent runtime.

132
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:41,000
 Memory forced me to separate the responsibilities around the runtime.

133
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:46,000
 Durable execution showed me which state really mattered to it.

134
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:54,000
 Comparing the candidates showed me that an agent framework can't be evaluated in isolation from the platform around it.

135
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
 There's another lesson here as well.

136
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:01,000
 Meeting requirements doesn't necessarily determine the architecture.

137
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
 Several of these approaches meet the requirement.

138
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:12,000
 The architectural decision emerges from the trade-offs between them, the boundaries, the responsibilities and the complexity.

139
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:16,000
 And the options they preserve for the future.

140
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,000
 So ADR1 is now accepted.

141
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:25,000
 Articulate has a runtime which architectural intelligence can eventually execute.

142
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
 But that creates and have a problem.

143
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:32,000
 Those agents are going to reason about architectural knowledge.

144
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
 They're going to discover new information.

145
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:37,000
 They're going to identify contradictions.

146
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,000
 They're going to make new claims about the architecture.

147
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:46,000
 And some of those claims may eventually need to change what articulate understands to be true.

148
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:54,000
 But an agent shouldn't simply be able to rewrite the knowledge model because it has reached a new conclusion.

149
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:59,000
 Architectural knowledge needs to evolve without losing where it came from.

150
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:04,000
 What it replaced or what it came to believe.

151
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:11,000
 So before I start implementing agents, there's one more part of the conceptual architecture I need to understand.

152
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:16,000
 How does architectural knowledge safely change over time?

153
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
 That's the question for the next episode.

154
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,000
 Knowledge Evolution.

